\fs96 \cf2 Using LayoutTensor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensor/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 LayoutTensor}} provides a view of multi-dimensional data stored in a linear array. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  abstracts the logical organization of multi-dimensional data from its actual arrangement in memory. You can generate new tensor "views" of the same data without copying the underlying data. This facilitates essential patterns for writing performant computational algorithms, such as:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Extracting tiles (sub-tensors) from existing tensors. This is especially valuable on the GPU, allowing a thread block to load a tile into shared memory, for faster access and more efficient caching.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Vectorizing tensors\'97reorganizing them into multi-element vectors for more performant memory loads and stores.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Partitioning a tensor into thread-local fragments to distribute work across a thread block.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is especially valuable for writing GPU kernels, and a number of its APIs are GPU-specific. However, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can also be used for CPU-based algorithms.\
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  consists of three main properties:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/layout/layouts/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 layout}}, defining how the elements are laid out in memory.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , defining the data type stored in the tensor.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A pointer to memory where the data is stored.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Figure 1 shows the relationship between the layout and the storage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 1. Layout and storage for a 2D tensor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Figure 1 shows a 2D column-major layout, and the corresponding linear array of storage. The values shown inside the layout are offsets into the storage: so the coordinates (0, 1) correspond to offset 2 in the storage.\
Because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a view, creating a new tensor based on an existing tensor doesn't require copying the underlying data. So you can easily create a new view, representing a tile (sub-tensor), or accessing the elements in a different order. These views all access the same data, so changing the stored data in one view changes the data seen by all of the views.\
Each element in a tensor can be either a single (scalar) value or a SIMD vector of values. For a vectorized layout, you can specify an element layout that determines how the vector elements are laid out in memory. For more information, see \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Vectorizing tensors\cf2 \ulnone .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Accessing tensor elements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 For tensors with simple row-major or column-major layouts, you can address a layout tensor like a multidimensional array to access elements:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 element \cf5 =\cf13  tensor2d\cf5 [\cf13 x\cf5 ,\cf13  y\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 tensor2d\cf5 [\cf13 x\cf5 ,\cf13  y\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  z\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The number of indices passed to the subscript operator must match the number of coordinates required by the tensor. For simple layouts, this is the same as the layout's rank: two for a 2D tensor, three for a 3D tensor, and so on. If the number of indices is incorrect, you may see a cryptic runtime error.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # Indexing into a 2D tensor requires two indices\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14 el1 \cf5 =\cf13  tensor2d\cf5 [\cf13 x\cf5 ,\cf13  y\cf5 ]\cf13   \cf6 # Works\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 el2 \cf5 =\cf13  tensor2d\cf5 [\cf13 x\cf5 ]\cf13   \cf6 # Runtime error\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more complicated "nested" layouts, such as tiled layouts, the number of indices doesn't match the rank of the tensor. For details, see \cf5 \ul Tensor indexing and nested layouts\cf2 \ulnone .\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __getitem__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method returns a SIMD vector of elements, and the compiler can't statically determine the size of the vector (which is the size of the tensor's element layout). This can cause type checking errors at compile time, because some APIs can only accept scalar values (SIMD vectors of width 1). For example, consider the following code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 i\cf5 :\cf13  Int \cf5 =\cf13  SIMD\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 int32\cf5 ,\cf13  width\cf5 ](\cf17 15\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 width
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is 1, the vector can be implicitly converted to an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but if 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 width
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is any other value, the vector can't be implicitly converted. If 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 width
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  isn't known at compile time, this produces an error.\
If your tensor stores scalar values, you can work around this by explicitly taking the first item in the vector:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  element \cf5 =\cf13  tensor\cf5 [\cf13 row\cf5 ,\cf13  col\cf5 ][\cf17 0\cf5 ]\cf13   \cf6 # element is guaranteed to be a scalar value\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can also access elements using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 load()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 store()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods, which let you specify the vector width explicitly:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  elements\cf5 :\cf13  SIMD\cf5 [\cf13 DType\cf5 .\cf13 float32\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf17 4\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  elements \cf5 =\cf13  tensor\cf5 .\cf13 load\cf5 [\cf17 4\cf5 ](\cf13 row\cf5 ,\cf13  col\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 elements \cf5 =\cf13  elements \cf5 *\cf13  \cf17 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 tensor\cf5 .\cf13 store\cf5 (\cf13 row\cf5 ,\cf13  col\cf5 ,\cf13  elements\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Tensor indexing and nested layouts\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A tensor's layout may have nested modes (or sub-layouts), as described in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/layout/layouts/#modes"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Introduction to layouts}}. These layouts have one or more of their dimensions divided into sub-layouts. For example, Figure 2 shows a tensor with a nested layout:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 2. Tensor with nested layout\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Figure 2 shows a tensor with a tile-major nested layout. Instead of being addressed with a single coordinate on each axis, it has a pair of coordinates per axis. For example, the coordinates 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ((1, 0), (0, 1))
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  map to the offset 6.\
You can't pass nested coordinates to the subscript operator 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 []
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , but you can pass a flattened version of the coordinates. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb14 # retrieve the value at ((1, 0), (0, 1))\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf13 \cb14 element \cf5 =\cf13  nested_tensor\cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf17 0\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf17 0\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf17 1\cf5 ][\cf17 0\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The number of indices passed to the subscript operator must match the flattened rank of the tensor.\
You can't currently use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 load()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 store()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods for tensors with nested layouts. However, these methods are usually used on tensors that have been \cf5 \ul tiled\cf2 \ulnone , which yields a tensor with a simple layout.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Creating a LayoutTensor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are several ways to create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , depending on where the tensor data resides:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On the CPU.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In GPU global memory.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In GPU shared or local memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In addition to methods for creating a tensor from scratch, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides a number of methods for producing a new view of an existing tensor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb18 No bounds checking\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb18 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb19 LayoutTensor
\fs28 \cf2 \cb18  constructors don't do any bounds-checking to verify that the allocated memory is large enough to hold all of the elements specified in the layout. It's up to the user to ensure that the proper amount of space is allocated.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Creating a 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  on the CPU\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 While 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is often used on the GPU, you can also use it to create tensors for use on the CPU.\
To create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for use on the CPU, you need a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Layout
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and a block of memory to store the tensor data. A common way to allocate memory for a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is to use an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/inline_array/InlineArray/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul InlineArray}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  rows \cf5 =\cf13  \cf17 8\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  columns \cf5 =\cf13  \cf17 16\cf13 \cb1 \
