\fs48 \cf2 Creating a 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  on the GPU\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When creating a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for use on the GPU, you need to consider which memory space the tensor data will be stored in:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
GPU global memory can only be allocated from the host (CPU), as a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
GPU shared or local memory can be statically allocated on the GPU.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Creating a 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  in global memory\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You must allocate global memory from the host side, by allocating a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceBuffer/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul DeviceBuffer}}. You can either construct a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  using this memory on the host side, before invoking a GPU kernel, or you can construct a LayoutTensor inside the kernel itself:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On the CPU, you can construct a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  using a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as its storage. Although you can create this tensor on the CPU and pass it in to a kernel function, you can't directly modify its values on the CPU, since the memory is on the GPU.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
On the GPU: When a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is passed in to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 enqueue_function()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the kernel receives a corresponding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 UnsafePointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in place of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DeviceBuffer.
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  The kernel can then create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 pointer
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In both cases, if you want to initialize data for the tensor from the CPU, you can call {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_copy"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul enqueue_copy()}} or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/gpu/host/device_context/DeviceContext/#enqueue_memset"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul enqueue_memset()}} on the buffer prior to invoking the kernel. The following example shows initializing a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  from the CPU and passing it to a GPU kernel.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

