\fs40 \cf2 Creating a 
\fs35 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs40 \cf2 \cb1  in shared or local memory\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To create a tensor on the GPU in shared memory or local memory, use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor.stack_allocation()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  static method to create a tensor with backing memory in the appropriate memory space.\
Both shared and local memory are very limited resources, so a common pattern is to copy a small tile of a larger tensor into shared memory or local memory to reduce memory access time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  tile_layout \cf5 =\cf13  Layout\cf5 .\cf13 row_major\cf5 (\cf17 16\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf17 16\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  shared_tile \cf5 =\cf13  LayoutTensor\cf5 [\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     dtype\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     tile_layout\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     MutAnyOrigin\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     address_space\cf5 =\cf13 AddressSpace\cf5 .\cf13 SHARED\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 ].\cf13 stack_allocation\cf5 ()\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In the case of shared memory, all threads in a thread block see the same allocation. For local memory, each thread gets a separate allocation.\
There's no way to explicitly allocate register memory. However, the compiler can promote some local memory allocations to registers. To enable this optimization, keep the size of the tensor small, and all indexing into the tensor static\'97for example, using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loops.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb18 The name 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb19 stack_allocation()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb18  is misleading. It is a static allocation, meaning the allocation is processed at compile time. The allocation is like a C/C++ stack allocation in that its lifetime ends when the function in which it was allocated returns. This API may be subject to change in the near future.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Tiling tensors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A fundamental pattern for using a layout tensor is to divide the tensor into smaller tiles to achieve better data locality and cache efficiency. In a GPU kernel you may want to select a tile that corresponds to the size of a thread block. For example, given a 2D thread block of 16x16 threads, you could use a 16x16 tile (with each thread handling one element in the tile) or a 64x16 tile (with each thread handling 4 elements from the tensor).\
Tiles are most commonly 1D or 2D. For element-wise calculations, where the output value for a given tensor element depends on only one input value, 1D tiles are easy to reason about. For calculations that involve neighboring elements, 2D tiles can help maintain data locality. For example, matrix multiplication or 2D convolution operations usually use 2D tiles.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  provides a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 tile()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method for extracting a single tile. You can also iterate through tiles using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensorIter
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type.\
When tiling a tensor that isn't an exact multiple of the tile size, you can create the tensor as a masked tensor (with the optional 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 masked
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter set to True). When tiling a masked tensor, the tile operations will return partial tiles at the edges of the tensor. These tiles will be smaller than the requested tile size. You can use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 tensor.dim(axis)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to query the tile dimensions at runtime.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Extracting a tile\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensor/#tile"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul LayoutTensor.tile()}} method extracts a tile with a given size at a given set of coordinates:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  rows \cf5 =\cf13  \cf17 2\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  columns \cf5 =\cf13  \cf17 4\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  tile_size \cf5 =\cf13  \cf17 32\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  tile_layout \cf5 =\cf13  Layout\cf5 .\cf13 row_major\cf5 (\cf13 tile_size\cf5 ,\cf13  tile_size\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  tiler_layout \cf5 =\cf13  Layout\cf5 .\cf13 row_major\cf5 (\cf13 rows\cf5 ,\cf13  columns\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  tiled_layout \cf5 =\cf13  blocked_product\cf5 (\cf13 tile_layout\cf5 ,\cf13  tiler_layout\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  storage \cf5 =\cf13  InlineArray\cf5 [\cf13 Float32\cf5 ,\cf13  tiled_layout\cf5 .\cf13 size\cf5 ()](\cf13 uninitialized\cf5 =\cf17 True\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf20 range\cf5 (comptime\cf13  \cf5 (\cf13 tiled_layout\cf5 .\cf13 size\cf5 ())):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     storage\cf5 [\cf13 i\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  Float32\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
