\fs48 \cf2 Tiled iterators\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensorIter/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul LayoutTensorIter}} struct provides a way to iterate through a block of memory, generating a layout tensor for each position. There are two ways to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensorIter
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Starting with a memory buffer, you can generate a series of tiles.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Given an existing layout tensor, you can extract a set of tiles along a given axis.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Tiling a memory buffer\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When you start with a memory buffer, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensorIter
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  iterates through the memory one tile at a time. This essentially treats the memory as a flat array of tiles.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

