\cf2 The iterator constructor takes all of the parameters you'd use to construct a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 DType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , layout, and an origin\'97and as arguments it takes a pointer and the size of the memory buffer.\
Note that the iterator doesn't work like a standard iterator, and you can't use it directly in a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement like you would use a collection. Instead, you can use either the dereference operator (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 iter[]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) or the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 get()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method to retrieve a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  representing the tile at the current position.\
You can advance the iterator by incrementing it, as shown above. The iterator also supports 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 next()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 next_unsafe()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods, which return a copy of the iterator incremented by a specified offset (default 1). This means you can also use a pattern like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf20 range\cf5 (\cf13 num_tiles\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     current_tile \cf5 =\cf13  \cf20 iter\cf5 .\cf20 next\cf5 (\cf13 i\cf5 )[]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \'85\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensorIter
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  also has an optional boolean 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 circular
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter. A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensorIter
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 created with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 circular=True
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  treats the memory buffer as circular; when it hits the end of the buffer, it starts over again at the beginning.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

