\fs64 \cf2 Partitioning a tensor across threads\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When working with tensors on the GPU, it's sometimes desirable to distribute the elements of a tensor across the threads in a thread block. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensor/#distribute"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul distribute()}} method takes a thread layout and a thread ID and returns a thread-specific fragment of the tensor.\
The thread layout is tiled across the tensor. The Nth thread receives a fragment consisting of the Nth value from each tile. For example, Figure 5 shows how 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 distribute()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  forms fragments given a 4x4, row-major tensor and a 2x2, column-major thread layout:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 5. Partitioning a tensor into fragments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In Figure 5, the numbers in the data layout represent offsets into storage, as usual. The numbers in the thread layout represent thread IDs.\
The example in Figure 5 uses a small thread layout for illustration purposes. In practice, it's usually optimal to use a thread layout that's the same size as the warp size of your GPU, so the work is divided across all available threads. For example, the following code vectorizes and partitions a tensor over a full warp worth of threads:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf13  thread_layout \cf5 =\cf13  Layout\cf5 .\cf13 row_major\cf5 (\cf13 WARP_SIZE \cf5 //\cf13  simd_size\cf5 ,\cf13  simd_size\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  fragment \cf5 =\cf13  tile\cf5 .\cf13 vectorize\cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf13  simd_size\cf5 ]().\cf13 distribute\cf5 [\cf13 thread_layout\cf5 ](\cf13 lane_id\cf5 ())\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf15 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Given a 16x16 tile size, a warp size of 32 and a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 simd_size
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  of 4, this code produces a 16x4 tensor of 1x4 vectors. The thread layout is an 8x4 row major layout.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Copying tensors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 layout-tensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  package provides a large set of utilities for copying tensors. A number of these are specialized for copying between various GPU memory spaces. All of the layout tensor copy methods respect the layouts\'97so you can transform a tensor by copying it to a tensor with a different layout.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 LayoutTensor
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  itself provides two methods for copying tensor data:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensor/#copy_from"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul copy_from()}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 copies data from a source tensor to the current tensor, which may be in a different memory space.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/kernels/layout/layout_tensor/LayoutTensor/#copy_from_async"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul copy_from_async()}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 is an optimized copy mechanism for asynchronously copying from GPU global memory to shared memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Both of these methods copy the entire source tensor. To divide the copying work among multiple threads, you need to use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 distribute()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to create thread-local tensor fragments, as described in \cf5 \ul Partitioning a tensor across threads\cf2 \ulnone .\
The following code sample demonstrates using both copy methods to copy data to and from shared memory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

